      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1128",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Noturus gladiator Thomas &amp; Burr, 2004</li><li>Common name: Piebald Madtom</li><li>Size: 107 mm (4.2\") SL</li><li>IUCN Red List: Near Threatened (last assessed 2012)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1128",
      "html": "<p>North America: coastal plain streams of Tennessee and Mississippi, USA. Gulf Coast drainages, Mississippi. Type locality: just upstream of Potters Creek, about 2.5 km west of Bolivar, Pleasant Run, Hatchie River at Summerville-Bolivar Road, Mississippi River basin, Hardeman County, Tennessee, U.S.A.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1128",
      "html": "<p>Breeding: unreported. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1128",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: from the Greek notos, meaning back, and oura, meaning tail, in reference to the confluent adipose and caudal fins. The specific epithet refers to the large, serrated pectoral spines (gladiator = swordsman).</p>"
    },
    {
